---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
import { loadPatternCatalog } from '../../../lib/pattern-catalog';

const patternCounts = loadPatternCatalog().counts ?? {};
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Detection Overview', href: '/docs/detection', active: true },
  { title: 'Detection Capabilities', href: '/docs/detection/capabilities' },
  { title: 'Seeded CVEs', href: '/docs/detection/cves' },
  { title: 'Suricata Network IDS', href: '/docs/detection/suricata' },
  { title: 'Pattern Database', href: '/docs/detection/patterns' },
  { title: 'Advanced Signals', href: '/docs/detection/advanced' },
  { title: 'Device & GDPR Signature', href: '/docs/detection/signature' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'pipeline', text: 'Detection Pipeline', level: 2 },
  { id: 'explore', text: 'Explore Detection', level: 2 },
  { id: 'coverage', text: 'Coverage Areas', level: 2 },
  { id: 'stages', text: 'Detection Stages', level: 2 },
  { id: 'thresholds', text: 'Thresholds & Scaling', level: 2 },
  { id: 'honest', text: 'Honest Assessment', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Detection" description="How the honeypot system detects and identifies attackers" section="Detection" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    Detection runs entirely inside the request path. Each non-static request is analysed by pure Lua rules,
    scored, and either forwarded to production or diverted to the session's honeypot pool. The same rules
    also produce the structured reasons used for logging and AbuseIPDB reporting.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pipeline">Detection Pipeline Overview</h2>
  <p>The honeypot detection pipeline ingests attacker traffic and produces actionable detection signals through a layered chain, implemented as a series of pure Lua modules (no <code>ngx.*</code> dependency, unit-testable under a plain Lua interpreter) driving an adapter half that performs I/O:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Traffic Capture</strong> — Nginx reverse proxy forwards HTTP requests; <code>pattern_analyzer.analyze_request()</code> runs once per non-static request.</li>
    <li><strong>Lua Pattern Matching</strong> — <code>pattern_rules.lua</code> and <code>vulnerability_rules.lua</code> match request URIs, headers, and arguments against seeded CVE exploit patterns.</li>
    <li><strong>Suricata Correlation</strong> — <code>suricata_rules.lua</code> parses <code>eve.json</code> alerts into structured records and converts them into risk-score deltas.</li>
    <li><strong>Risk Scoring</strong> — <code>pattern_analyzer.lua</code> accumulates scores; <code>router.lua</code> accumulates onto a decaying session base.</li>
    <li><strong>Routing Decision</strong> — <code>router.lua</code>'s 11-stage decision pipeline diverts high-risk requests to isolated honeypot pool instances.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting</strong> — <code>abuseipdb_rules.lua</code> gates external AbuseIPDB reporting and enriches comments with matched CVEs.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="explore">Explore Detection</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard
      title="Detection Capabilities"
      href="/docs/detection/capabilities"
      icon="target"
      tag="What is detected"
      description="URI, header and argument patterns, automation detection, upload analysis, honeytokens and sophistication classification."
      meta="Technique + module tables"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Seeded CVEs"
      href="/docs/detection/cves"
      icon="shieldAlert"
      tag="What is seeded"
      description="The explicitly matched WordPress plugin CVEs, the Suricata alert path and the AbuseIPDB reason-to-category mapping."
      meta="14+ CVEs · 6 reason types"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Suricata Network IDS"
      href="/docs/detection/suricata"
      icon="network"
      tag="Network layer"
      description="SID allocation, the OWASP WSTG category index and the eve.json parsing and severity-to-score contract."
      meta="70+ SIDs"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Pattern Database"
      href="/docs/detection/patterns"
      icon="search"
      tag="One catalogue"
      description="Every CVE, generic signature, upload and header rule, scanner tool and Suricata SID, with provenance, scores and attack lessons."
      meta={`${patternCounts.total ?? 0} patterns · ${patternCounts.cves ?? 0} CVEs · ${patternCounts.suricata_sids ?? 0} SIDs`}
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Advanced Signals"
      href="/docs/detection/advanced"
      icon="activity"
      tag="Scoring details"
      description="Vulnerability patterns, threat-alert processing, reportability rules, the scoring model and escalation-aware decay."
      meta="Decay policy"
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Device & GDPR Signature"
      href="/docs/detection/signature"
      icon="fingerprint"
      tag="Identity recovery"
      description="A consent-gated, one-way signature that re-recognises a returning visitor after a cookie and storage wipe, without storing anything about them."
      meta="Privacy-preserving"
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="coverage">Coverage Areas</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard
      title="Direct CVE detections"
      icon="shieldAlert"
      description="Plugin-specific and generic WordPress vulnerability patterns matched against URI, arguments and specific headers."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Generic attack patterns"
      icon="code"
      description="SQL injection, XSS, command injection, path traversal, file inclusion, deserialization, template and code injection."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Network IDS detections"
      icon="network"
      description="Suricata signatures aligned to OWASP WSTG categories, converted into severity-graded score deltas."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Behavioural signals"
      icon="activity"
      description="Brute force, scanning, automation, request-rate anomalies and per-session history accumulation."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Sophistication classification"
      icon="fingerprint"
      description="Weighted signals classify traffic as scripted, AI-assisted, manual or unknown."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Honeytokens"
      icon="key"
      description="Injected fake credentials are scanned for in inbound requests; a hit confirms compromise with high confidence."
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="stages">Detection Stages</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Analyser</th><th>WSTG</th><th>Score Impact</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>URI pattern analysis</td><td>WSTG-INPV, CONF, INFO</td><td>+15 base per generic; +5..+80 per specific</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Header analysis</td><td>WSTG-ATHN, AUTHZ</td><td>+50 flagged UA; +50 CVE header; +10 missing UA</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>CVE pattern matching</td><td>WSTG-AUTHZ, INPV</td><td>+40 per CVE matched</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>IP reputation + rate</td><td>—</td><td>+variable (threat_intel); +20 high rate</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>Request method</td><td>WSTG-INPV</td><td>+10 OPTIONS/TRACE; +15 override</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>Automation detection</td><td>WSTG-INFO, ATHN</td><td>+5..+30 per tool; +15 rapid streak</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6b</td><td>AJAX upload analysis</td><td>WSTG-INPV-10</td><td>Variable (upload_rules)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>Prompt-injection probe</td><td>—</td><td>+filter risk/2</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="thresholds">Thresholds & Scaling</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Honeypot threshold</strong>: 80 (raised from 50 to reduce false positives)</li>
    <li><strong>Max risk score</strong>: 100 (hard cap)</li>
    <li><strong>IP reputation cap</strong>: 10 per request on shared (NAT) addresses</li>
    <li><strong>Decay half-life</strong>: 300s, scaled by offense count via <code>decay_policy.lua</code></li>
    <li><strong>Permaflag</strong>: After 5 offenses or a single request scoring 100</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Multi-layered detection (11+ stages), explainable scoring, severity-graded Suricata integration, decay system prevents permanent poisoning, NAT protection, sophistication classification.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> Same-host Suricata testing gap (unverified against genuine external traffic), threshold tuning needed for legitimate API clients, generic pattern false positives (historical), decay complexity (4 tunable params), honeytoken coverage (only 5 types), no ML, Suricata rule maintenance (70+ SIDs).</p>
</DocsLayout>